Every zero-tolerance class recall is exactly 100%, computed per-class with no
averaging, consistent with the ledger's Freeze Record pre-replay reconciliation
table.

### Family / Source Binding

All 18 required roadmap families are covered by at least one case (two
families carry two cases for redundant zero-tolerance coverage: expected/
actual manifest omission and unauthorized path; worker-modified verifier used
for self-acceptance). Every case binds to one of the seven pinned sources
(`H0`, `P1`, `P2`, `GCLH`, `WEBUX`, `CADP`, `LATENCY`). Fourteen cases are
`HISTORICAL_OUTCOME` (the fixture asserts a real documented historical
result); six cases are `SEEDED_DERIVATION` (a synthetic mutation layered on a
real source for zero-tolerance coverage the historical record does not
itself contain, each labeled honestly with a non-`none` `mutation` field).

### First-Run Versus Final-Run Divergence (Full Disclosure)

The first replay invocation reported `RETURN_TO_DESIGN` with one false
positive: `MFRP-P3-C11` (source `GCLH`, family
`unrecognized_hash_byte_domain_or_normalization_recipe`, `zeroToleranceClass:
NONE`). Root cause: the helper's `_evaluate_case` function required every
`NONE`-class case to resolve to a bare clean result
(`VALID_NO_MUTATION_NEEDED`), but `MFRP-P3-C11`'s frozen
`expectedReceiptResult` is `MANIFEST_GAP_SURFACED` because the case honestly
encodes GCLH-RV-3's documented byte-domain residual, which is a real,
correctly-surfaced finding that is not a zero-tolerance class under the
roadmap. This is not a case where the fixture's expectation was wrong; it is
a case where the helper's binary clean/non-clean contract was too narrow for
a legitimate non-zero-tolerance family that still surfaces a gap. The repair
was made only in `governance/compat/mfrp_historical_replay.py`
(`_evaluate_case`): a `NONE`-class case is now accepted when its expectation
is either a recognized clean result or a recognized conservative-surface
result. Neither `governance/compat/fixtures/mfrp_p3_historical_replay.json`
nor `docs/reviews/CVF_MFRP_P3_FROZEN_DEFECT_AND_FALSE_NEGATIVE_LEDGER_2026-09-01.md`
Freeze Record content was edited before or after this repair: disposition
`MATCH`, confirmed by `python -c "hashlib.sha256(open(fixture,'rb').read()).hexdigest()"`
rehashed immediately before the final replay run and compared against the
ledger's recorded `corpusSha256`.

### Ledger Post-Freeze Administrative Addition Disclosure

After the frozen fixture and ledger passed the first and final replay with
`REPLAY_PASS_CANDIDATE`, `run_worker_return_fast_gate.py` reported that the
ledger (a `docs/reviews/` artifact) was missing required governance-gate
administrative sections: the checker-read-ahead section, the agent-operation-
trace section, the epistemic-process section, and a `Core Guard
Self-Protection Authorization` block covering the two new Python paths. These
four sections were added to the ledger after the freeze point recorded in its
own Freeze Record. This is disclosed here explicitly for reviewer judgment:
the addition did not change the ledger's Freeze Record, Source Verification
Block, Family And Zero-Tolerance Reconciliation table, or any expected-outcome
content; it added only administrative governance metadata that the fast gate
requires of every `docs/reviews/` artifact regardless of freeze status. No
corpus hash, case-set digest, case expectation, or zero-tolerance recall
figure was touched. The two literal-format repairs made inside those new
administrative sections (removing a self-triggering backtick-quoted heading
per gotcha 5, and replacing a non-parseable `Actual changed set` prose value
with literal paths) are also confined to those same new administrative
sections.

## Risk / Corrective Action

| Risk | Corrective action |
|---|---|
| fixture or ledger tuned after first replay to manufacture a pass | Freeze Record and Source Verification Block in the ledger are unedited from their original freeze; only administrative sections were later appended, disclosed above; the fixture's corpus SHA-256 was independently reconfirmed unchanged immediately before the final replay |
| seeded mutation misrepresented as historical | every `SEEDED_DERIVATION` case carries a non-`none` `mutation` field; the test suite asserts this invariant (`test_seeded_derivation_cases_are_labeled_honestly`) |
| zero-tolerance averaging | the helper reports recall per class as a `detected/total` pair; the test suite asserts every class individually reaches 100% (`test_zero_tolerance_recall_is_100_percent_for_every_class`) |
| helper emitting `REPLAY_PASS` | the helper's terminal result is restricted to `REPLAY_PASS_CANDIDATE` or `RETURN_TO_DESIGN`; the string `REPLAY_PASS` (without `_CANDIDATE`) does not appear in the helper source, asserted by `test_helper_never_emits_bare_replay_pass_literal` |
| helper write/subprocess/network behavior | the helper performs no `subprocess`, `urllib`, `socket`, or file-write call; asserted by `test_helper_is_read_only_and_defines_no_write_or_subprocess_calls` |
| P2 production surface regression | `test_agent_autorun_machine_verification.py` and `test_agent_automation_machine_verification_readout.py` were rerun unmodified and pass 18/18; neither file's content changed in this batch |

## Decision / Disposition

`COMPLETE_PENDING_REVIEW`. First and final replay evidence, freeze chronology,
zero-tolerance recall, and the exact five-path changed set are recorded above
for independent reviewer verification. The reviewer alone may select
`REPLAY_PASS`; this worker return does not claim it. `successorTrancheOpened:
NO`; P4-P6 remain unopened.

